  "id": "chunk_16",
  "chunk": "Instead of deploying an RL approach that trains agents through millions of\nepisodes on a specific game, we examine LLMs in a zero-shot setting—mirroring\nhow humans can pick up and play a new game without extensive prior training.\nBy examining where and how these models succeed or fail, we aim to shed\nlight on current limitations in LLMs’ reasoning, spatial understanding, memory\nutilization, and metacognitive capabilities.\n3 Methodology\nWe conduct our experiments usingZork I: The Great Underground Empire, the\nfirst instalment of the commercially released Zork trilogy, originally developed\nat MIT in 1977 and later released by Infocom in 1980. While all games in\nthe trilogy are considered challenging, the sequels (Zork IIandZork III) are\narguably more difficult, making Zork I a suitable starting point for this research.\nThe game is accessed through a browser-based implementation [14]. A custom-\nbuilt Python script connects the LLM and the game environment. The script",
